---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="One Dataset, Many Interpretations | tcplot: Data Visualization in Minneapolis and St. Paul"
    description="Everyone gets the same never-before-seen dataset at the same moment. Build what you see in it, then share how differently we all read the same numbers."
>
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8">
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-8 mb-12">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">
                Past Event: One Dataset, Many Interpretations — September 26th
            </h2>

            <div class="mb-6 text-gray-700">
                <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">
                    Saturday, Sep 26 · 10:00 AM to 12:00 PM CDT
                </p>
                <p class="flex items-start gap-2 mt-1">
                    <svg
                        class="w-5 h-5 mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-gray-400"
                        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                        fill="currentColor"
                        aria-hidden="true"
                    >
                        <path
                            fill-rule="evenodd"
                            d="M12 2c-3.866 0-7 3.07-7 6.857 0 4.34 4.686 9.86 6.412 11.74a.8.8 0 0 0 1.176 0C14.314 18.717 19 13.197 19 8.857 19 5.07 15.866 2 12 2Zm0 9.5a2.5 2.5 0 1 1 0-5 2.5 2.5 0 0 1 0 5Z"
                            clip-rule="evenodd"></path>
                    </svg>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >The Coffee Shop NE</span
                        ><br />2852 Johnson St NE · Minneapolis, MN</span
                    >
                </p>
            </div>

            <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                One dataset. A room full of ways to see it.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Nobody sees the dataset until the clock starts. Then everyone
                gets the same data at the same moment, with total freedom of
                tools: build whatever you see in it, share it in sixty
                seconds, and discover how differently the room read the same
                numbers.
            </p>

            <div class="flex justify-center mt-12 mb-8">
                <div class="max-w-2xl w-full rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
                    <img
                        src="/images/maple-canopy.jpeg"
                        alt="Looking up through a canopy of backlit green maple leaves against a blue sky"
                        class="w-full h-auto object-contain"
                    />
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                The dataset: City of Minneapolis Public Tree Inventory
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                A map of Minneapolis public trees, each with a genus or species
                and a latitude and longitude. Despite its title, it is not
                published by the City of Minneapolis or the Park Board. The
                owner is a private ArcGIS account belonging to an independent
                Minneapolis researcher. By that researcher's own description,
                the main layer is the University of Minnesota MSP-LTER 2023
                urban tree inventory and the planting layers were extracted
                from Park Board carbon credit filings; we have not verified
                either against the original sources. It is big, it is uneven,
                and it will not tell you what to look at. That is the point.
            </p>
            <p>
                <a
                    href="https://www.arcgis.com/home/item.html?id=9e896259239d428dbf332a2ac595b623"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer"
                    class="inline-block bg-blue-600 text-white font-semibold px-5 py-3 rounded-lg hover:bg-blue-700 transition-colors"
                    >Open the data on ArcGIS</a
                >
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</Layout>
